{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_1","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"cat docs/design/datom.world.md && rg -n 'emit\"'!|emit-reply!|client-mode!|defn -main|:ready'\"' test/yin/repl/slice_peer.cljc && ls -l build/yin-repl-peer build/tools/jolt-env.sh /tmp/p1dr2*.py /tmp/p1dr3-*timings.json; ls -ld build/gambit-*; tail -8 /Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/jolt/1795187823308-engineer-p1d-uniform-launcher.gpt-6.2-sol.findings.md\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_1","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"cat docs/design/datom.world.md && rg -n 'emit\"'!|emit-reply!|client-mode!|defn -main|:ready'\"' test/yin/repl/slice_peer.cljc && ls -l build/yin-repl-peer build/tools/jolt-env.sh /tmp/p1dr2*.py /tmp/p1dr3-*timings.json; ls -ld build/gambit-*; tail -8 /Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/jolt/1795187823308-engineer-p1d-uniform-launcher.gpt-6.2-sol.findings.md\"","aggregated_output":"---\ndescription: Core philosophy, foundational axioms, invariants, primitives (tuples, streams, host boundaries), architectural commitments, design principles, code style, and development workflow for datom.world\n---\n\n# DATOM.WORLD ARCHITECTURAL FOUNDATIONS & DEVELOPMENT RULES\n\n## Core Philosophy\n\nEverything is data.\nInterpretation > abstraction.\nExplicit causality > implicit assumptions.\nRestrictions are a feature.\n\n### Foundational Axioms\n\n1. **Everything is a Stream**: Portable I/O and system data flow through append-only streams; hosts may also install explicit local functions that perform direct I/O.\n2. **Interpretation Creates Semantics**: Data is syntax; semantics only emerge through interpretation (one truth, many perspectives).\n   At system scale, a \"system\" is an emergent property of interpreters of\n   pure data, not an object, service, or stored artifact. Find the\n   interpreters and the data they share to find the system.\n3. **Code and State are Datoms**: Code (Universal AST) and runtime execution state are structured datoms, enabling syntax independence and structural reflection.\n4. **Everything is a Continuation**: Computation is modeled as serializable, portable continuations that can pause, travel across streams, and resume anywhere.\n\n## Non-Negotiable Invariants\n\n- Do not introduce hidden global state.\n- Do not introduce implicit control flow.\n- Do not introduce callbacks; every callback is an event on a stream.\n- Do not introduce shared mutable state.\n- Do not collapse interpretation and execution into the same layer.\n- Do not assume graphs: graphs must be constructed explicitly from tuples.\n- Do not lose information (unitarity): streams are append-only and no datom is destroyed, only reinterpreted. The Universal AST is never lost: it is always retrievable wherever code is lowered, by address or by a query over its datoms. Every derived form (semantic vector, native images, indexes) is an interpretation of it and may drop facts only because the AST remains.\n\n## Tuples and Datoms\n\nTuples are elements in an open moduli space, graded by dimension $n$. A tuple can be any dimension/size.\nA datom is the canonical persistent tuple `[e a v t m]` (entity, attribute, value, transaction, metadata). Persistent facts are immutable datoms.\nSource scope belongs to the interpreter, never to an appended tuple slot. Queries keep physical sources as separate db-values and express union or joins explicitly.\n\nSee [`docs/design/datom.md`](./datom.md) for full specification.\n\n## Streams\n\n`dao.stream` is an I/O monad and the system's portable I/O ABI. Functions consume streams and produce streams.\nHosts may install arbitrary local functions, including direct I/O; their availability and migration semantics must be explicit.\nPortable behavior cannot assume them; portable side effects appear as stream emissions. Direct-I/O host modules are explicitly local and non-migratable (owner decision, 2026-10-23).\nStreams are values that can be sent through streams.\nStreams carry whatever values the consumer needs: no datom requirement, no privileged payload shape.\n\nA stream has no privileged reader, and its meaning is not in it. Any number\nof interpreters may observe the same medium, and each constructs its own\nsemantics from the same batches: a `yin.vm` evaluator observing a program\nstream constructs CESK state and executes it; `dao.space.index` observing\nthe very same stream materializes covered indexes, so `dao.space.query/q`\nanswers Datalog over the program as it grows. Neither knows the other\nexists. The medium is their only coupling, and each observer's ignorance of\nthe other is load-bearing: the evaluator never consults an index to run,\nand the index never evaluates a form to index it. This is how axiom 2\nbecomes architecture: one stream, many interpretations, none of them\nthe stream's own.\n\nFunctions can take anything and return anything.\nAgents are functions, closures, or continuations in `yin.vm` that communicate portably with the outside world through `dao.stream`. Portable agent behavior is specified through stream effects; host-local functions may also perform direct I/O.\n\nSee [`docs/design/dao.stream.md`](./dao.stream.md) for full stream specification.\nSee [`docs/design/yin.vm.streams-all-the-way-down.md`](./yin.vm.streams-all-the-way-down.md) for VM architecture.\n\n### Host Boundaries\n\nA host boundary is the dao.stream abstraction boundary; implementations do not add contracts.\n[datom.world.abi.md](./datom.world.abi.md) owns the doctrine, owner rulings, taxonomy, floor and custody rules.\n\n## Architectural Commitments\n\n- **`dao.jing` is syntax, agents are semantics**: the content-addressed encoding stays passive and payload-agnostic.\n- **Macros are stream topology**: an evaluator knows nothing about macro expansion; expansion happens on the syntax side of a medium boundary, upstream of any evaluator.\n- **Peer observers, one stream**: a generic evaluator and `dao.space` independently observe the same stream topic, as described under Streams. This is not a claim about any two consumers regardless of what they consume.\n- **Observers are independent and optional**: nothing requires a medium to have any particular set of consumers. Which observers exist for a stream is a per-composition choice, not a design-time commitment.\n\n## Design Principles\n\nCode quality is measured by malleability: how cheaply can changes adapt without breaking promises?\nStigmergic coordination emerges from sharing data across streams.\n\n**Systems emerge from interpreters of pure data.** Streams are the\ncomposition medium; each interpreter gives the same data a perspective\nwithout collapsing interpretation into execution or owning the truth.\n\n- `dao.space` emerges from `dao.space.index` and `dao.space.query` over\n  ordinary streams.\n- ShiBi capability, metering, and budget emerge from different\n  interpreters over the same tuples; no token object is stored.\n- The four VMs interpret one Universal AST; many syntaxes feed that AST.\n- The semantic AST in `dao.space` is queryable facts projected by the\n  auto-index observer.\n- Client and server are roles in one stigmergic behavior implemented by\n  interpreters, not distinct kinds of nodes.\n\n**Derive, don't persist.** Before adding a tag, slot, or field to any\ncanonical content-hashed structure (an AST grammar, a datom schema, a row\nshape), check whether a correctly scoped Datalog query over\n`dao.space.query/q` can already compute the fact. If it can, it belongs as a\nquery or projection: new persisted structure migrates every downstream\nconsumer and duplicates authority over a fact a query derives fresh. The\nretired `:global` tag in\n[`docs/design/yin.vm.code-as-tuples.md`](./yin.vm.code-as-tuples.md) is the\ncase study, including the scoping bug that a naive query introduced.\n\nSee [`docs/agents/malleability.md`](../agents/malleability.md) for complete design guidance (cohesion, coupling, bounded complexity, evolution, testing strategy).\nSee [`docs/agents/vocabulary.md`](../agents/vocabulary.md) for domain vocabulary reference (biology, economics, physics, philosophy).\n\n## Code Style\n\n- Prefer simple data over rich types.\n- Prefer total functions over partial ones.\n- Prefer declarative pipelines over imperative logic.\n- Avoid cleverness.\n- Name things after what they do to streams.\n\n## Development Workflow\n\n- Do not invent abstractions that hide streams.\n- Do not suggest mainstream frameworks unless explicitly asked.\n- Do not optimize prematurely.\n- When uncertain, ask at the architectural level, not the implementation level.\n- Explanations should align with Plan 9 / Datomic / Lisp lineage.\n- Program store writes go through `engine/store-put`; any other store write\n  the store-write audit (`test/yin/vm/store_write_audit_test.clj`)\n  detects must be on its allowlist; its docstring states the residual it\n  leaves to review.\n\n### Problem Decomposition\n\nIf a problem appears complex:\n1. Decompose it into streams.\n2. Identify invariants.\n3. Restrict degrees of freedom.\n4. Make causality explicit.\n5. Let structure emerge from constraints.\n\n## Operational & Specialized Guides\n\n- **Advanced Concepts**: See [`docs/agents/advanced-concepts.md`](../agents/advanced-concepts.md) for parallel transport, capability tokens, and entanglement.\n- **Build & Test**: See [`docs/agents/build-n-test.md`](../agents/build-n-test.md) for build, lint, and test commands and TDD guidelines.\n- **Engineering Team Roster**: See [`docs/agents/team.md`](../agents/team.md) for engineering team roles and LLM model assignments.\n- **Agent Delegation**: See [`docs/agents/roles/orchestrator.md`](../agents/roles/orchestrator.md) for collaboration protocols and delegating tasks or reviews through `agy`, `cmd`, `glm`, `deepseek`, and `codex`; each orchestrator session has its own append-only log at `orchestrator-logs/<unix-time-stamp>-<brief-orchestrator-descriptive-name>.md` under the repository root.\n- **Website & Content**: See [`docs/agents/format.md`](../agents/format.md) for `.chp` and `.blog` EDN/Hiccup file specifications.\n94:(defn- emit!\n108:(defn- emit-reply!\n110:  (emit! (transit/encode value)))\n266:    :events (do (emit-reply! {:reply :events :events (take-atom! events)})\n269:              (emit-reply! {:reply :line :accepted true})\n272:               (emit-reply! (probe box first-traffic health))\n279:               (emit-reply!\n287:    (do (emit-reply! {:reply :error :command (pr-str (:cmd command))})\n291:(defn- client-mode!\n298:    (emit-reply! {:reply :ready :booted true})\n327:                    (emit! (get entry serve/text-key)))\n350:(defn -main\n355:    (client-mode!)))\nls: build/yin-repl-peer: No such file or directory\n-rw-r--r--@ 1 sto  wheel  3424 Nov 20 23:09 /tmp/p1dr2-node-dart.py\n-rw-r--r--@ 1 sto  wheel  2822 Nov 20 23:11 /tmp/p1dr2-warm-sandbox.py\n-rw-r--r--@ 1 sto  wheel   275 Nov 21 13:27 /tmp/p1dr3-cold-timings.json\n-rw-r--r--@ 1 sto  wheel   160 Nov 21 13:27 /tmp/p1dr3-warm-timings.json\nlrwxr-xr-x@ 1 sto  staff    24 Nov 21 13:06 build/tools/jolt-env.sh -> ../p1d-tools/jolt-env.sh\ndrwxr-xr-x@ 4 sto  staff  128 Nov 21 13:10 build/gambit-ffi\ndrwxr-xr-x@ 7 sto  staff  224 Nov 21 13:15 build/gambit-yin\n- `bb bootstrap:jolt` verified pinned Jolt `f22df3c7` and all seven submodules,\n  printed `jolt smoke (+ 1 2) => 3`, and exited 0.\n- Both `bb smoke:jolt-target` targets reported the expected child identity\n  and result `3` and exited 0.\n- `cljstyle check` and `git diff --check` passed. The shared launcher retains\n  the pinned Linux fixture link flags. Runtime verification covered macOS.\n\nDONE\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_2","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"git status --short --branch && git log -3 --oneline && cat src/dev/yin_repl_launch.clj && cat src/dev/jolt_launch.clj | sed -n '139,174p'\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_2","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"git status --short --branch && git log -3 --oneline && cat src/dev/yin_repl_launch.clj && cat src/dev/jolt_launch.clj | sed -n '139,174p'\"","aggregated_output":"## feat/p1d-uniform-launcher...origin/master [ahead 1]\ne0ddd815 fix(repl): cache and report Gambit FFI bridge builds\n41ab9b38 docs(yin.repl): document the uniform bb yin-repl launcher — five backends, per-backend capability notes, jolt-gambit degraded scope; P1d review artifacts\n48c3d5bd merge: feat/p1d-uniform-launcher (c46d90ba) — uniform yin.repl launcher: bb yin-repl --backend jvm|node|dart|jolt-chez|jolt-gambit; per-backend prereq builds artifact-gated (node 2.9s, dart 0.15s warm); gambit interactive PTY session with the compiled-boot cache; capability notes honest per C4. Plus standalone waitset bugfix (e9fe4f10): bind POSIX read citing upstream e20e2174. Review: agy flash ACCEPT-WITH-FIXES -> r2 -> CONFIRMED-CLOSED (cross-family). Tri-host gate: 20 JVM failures all pre-existing at master tip b22dff6f (verified per-namespace G0); Node/Dart legs completed\n(ns yin-repl-launch\n  (:require [babashka.fs :as fs]\n            [babashka.process :as p]\n            [build-cache :as cache]\n            [clojure.string :as str]\n            [gambit-ffi-cache :as ffi]\n            [jolt-launch :as jolt]))\n\n\n(def ^:private backends #{\"jvm\" \"node\" \"dart\" \"jolt-chez\" \"jolt-gambit\"})\n\n\n(def ^:private gambit-capabilities\n  \"Local --index-store mem session; no :yang/python frontend or WebSocket serving.\")\n\n\n(def ^:private gambit-blocking\n  \"Blocking FFI refuses inline: blocking ffi is unsupported on the gambit target.\")\n\n\n(def ^:private usage\n  (str \"Usage: bb yin-repl [--backend jvm|node|dart|jolt-chez|jolt-gambit] [yin args...]\n\n  jvm          Default. Shipped JVM shell with Python and PHP frontends.\n  node         Shipped Node shell with Python and PHP frontends.\n  dart         Dart cross-host peer with a Transit command protocol.\n  jolt-chez    Pinned Jolt shell with the native Python frontend.\n  jolt-gambit  \" gambit-capabilities \"\n               \" gambit-blocking \"\n\nUse -- --help after the backend to pass --help to the selected program.\"))\n\n\n(defn- parse-launch\n  [args]\n  (let [[backend args] (cond\n                         (= \"--backend\" (first args)) [(second args) (nnext args)]\n                         (some-> (first args) (str/starts-with? \"--backend=\"))\n                         [(subs (first args) (count \"--backend=\")) (next args)]\n                         :else [\"jvm\" args])]\n    (when-not (backends backend)\n      (throw (ex-info (str \"Unknown or missing --backend \" (pr-str backend)\n                           \". Run bb yin-repl --help.\") {:exit 2})))\n    [backend args]))\n\n\n(defn- checked-shell\n  [opts & command]\n  (let [result (apply p/shell (merge {:continue true :shutdown nil} opts) command)]\n    (when-not (zero? (:exit result))\n      (throw (ex-info (str \"yin-repl prerequisite failed: \" (str/join \" \" command))\n                      {:exit (:exit result)})))\n    result))\n\n\n(defn- task!\n  [task & args]\n  (apply checked-shell {} \"bb\" task args))\n\n\n(defn- terminal-exit\n  [command args]\n  (:exit (apply p/shell {:in :inherit :continue true :shutdown nil}\n                (into command args))))\n\n\n(defn- ensure-jolt!\n  []\n  (when-not (fs/regular-file? \"build/tools/jolt-env.sh\")\n    (task! \"bootstrap:jolt\")))\n\n\n(defn- ensure-java-parsers!\n  []\n  (when-not (and (fs/regular-file? \"build/antlr/python3/classes/yang/python/antlr/gen/Python3Parser.class\")\n                 (fs/regular-file? \"build/antlr/php/classes/yang/php/antlr/gen/PhpParser.class\"))\n    (task! \"gen:antlr\" \"--\" \"--target\" \"java\")))\n\n\n(defn- ensure-chez-native!\n  []\n  (let [darwin? (str/includes? (str/lower-case (System/getProperty \"os.name\")) \"mac\")\n        arm64? (#{\"aarch64\" \"arm64\"} (System/getProperty \"os.arch\"))\n        platform (str (if darwin? \"darwin-\" \"linux-\") (if arm64? \"arm64\" \"x86_64\"))\n        extension (if darwin? \".dylib\" \".so\")]\n    (when-not (every? #(fs/regular-file? (str \"build/native/\" platform \"/jolt-\" %\n                                              \"/lib/libjolt_\" % extension))\n                      [\"crypto\" \"file\" \"parser\"])\n      (task! \"build:jolt-native\"))))\n\n\n(defn- jolt-session\n  [target extra-env command args]\n  (apply jolt/run-terminal-child\n         {:extra-env extra-env\n          :identity-prefix jolt/identity-prefix\n          :assert-identity! #(jolt/assert-child-target! {:target target :output %\n                                                         :context \"yin-repl\"})}\n         \"sh\" \"-c\" command \"jolt\" args))\n\n\n(defn- gambit-session\n  [args]\n  (ensure-jolt!)\n  (let [[jolt-home gsi] (-> (checked-shell {:out :string}\n                                           \"sh\" \"-c\" (jolt/toolchain-command\n                                                       \"printf '%s\\\\n' \\\"$JOLT_HOME\\\" \\\"$JOLT_GAMBIT\\\"\"))\n                            :out\n                            str/split-lines)\n        prefix (str (fs/parent (fs/parent gsi)))\n        cache-dir (fs/absolutize \"build/gambit-yin\")\n        _ (cache/local-path! cache-dir)\n        ffi-entry (ffi/ensure! jolt-home prefix)\n        _ (.println System/err \"jolt-gambit: Checking compiled Yin boot (a cold build can take several minutes)\")\n        boot (-> (checked-shell {:dir jolt-home :out :string}\n                                \"python3\" \"host/gambit/build-yin-boot.py\"\n                                (str (fs/path prefix \"bin/gsc\")) (str cache-dir))\n                 :out str/trim)\n        runtime (ffi/stage-runtime! cache-dir jolt-home boot ffi-entry)\n        driver (str (fs/path jolt-home \"host/gambit/yin-repl-native.ss\"))\n        identity \"(begin (display \\\"jolt-target: gambit\\\" (current-error-port)) (newline (current-error-port)) (force-output (current-error-port)))\"]\n    (.println System/err (str \"jolt-gambit  \" gambit-capabilities))\n    (.println System/err gambit-blocking)\n    (.println System/err \"jolt-gambit: Loading Yin namespaces (can take several minutes)\")\n    (jolt-session :gambit {\"JOLT_GAMBIT_YIN_BOOT\" boot}\n                  (jolt/launch-command {:target :gambit :cd? true})\n                  (into [runtime \"-e\" identity driver (str (fs/absolutize \".\"))] args))))\n\n\n(defn- launch\n  [backend args]\n  (case backend\n    \"jvm\" (do (ensure-java-parsers!)\n              (terminal-exit [\"clj\" \"-M:clj-yin-repl\"] args))\n    \"node\" (do (when-not (fs/regular-file? \"target/yin-repl.js\")\n                 (task! \"build:yin-repl-node\"))\n               (terminal-exit [\"node\" \"target/yin-repl.js\"] args))\n    \"dart\" (do (when-not (fs/regular-file? \"build/yin-repl-peer\")\n                 (task! \"build:yin-repl-peer\"))\n               (terminal-exit [\"build/yin-repl-peer\"] args))\n    \"jolt-chez\" (do (ensure-jolt!)\n                    (ensure-chez-native!)\n                    (jolt/preflight! :chez \"yin-repl\")\n                    (terminal-exit [\"sh\" \"-c\" (jolt/launch-command {:target :chez :alias \"jolt-yin-repl\"}) \"jolt\"]\n                                   args))\n    \"jolt-gambit\" (gambit-session args)))\n\n\n(let [exit (try\n             (let [[backend args] (parse-launch *command-line-args*)]\n               (if (#{\"-h\" \"--help\"} (first args))\n                 (do (println usage) (flush) 0)\n                 (launch backend (if (= \"--\" (first args)) (rest args) args))))\n             (catch Exception e\n               (.println System/err (ex-message e))\n               (or (:exit (ex-data e)) 1)))]\n  (System/exit exit))\n                      {:wanted target :observed observed})))\n    target))\n\n\n(defn run-terminal-child\n  \"Run a REPL with terminal stdin and stdout. Relay stderr and validate\n   the child's startup identity there before returning its exit status.\"\n  [{:keys [extra-env identity-prefix assert-identity!]} & command]\n  (let [child (apply p/process {:in :inherit :out :inherit :err :stream\n                                :extra-env extra-env}\n                     command)]\n    (try\n      (with-open [err (io/reader (:err child))]\n        (let [identity (loop []\n                         (when-let [line (.readLine err)]\n                           (.println System/err line)\n                           (if (str/starts-with? line identity-prefix)\n                             line\n                             (recur))))]\n          (assert-identity! identity))\n        (io/copy err System/err)\n        (.flush System/err))\n      (:exit @child)\n      (finally\n        (when (p/alive? child)\n          (p/destroy child))))))\n\n\n;; The child-side identity print, for repo scripts that run UNDER jolt (where\n;; the bb seam namespace cannot load): scripts embed this one line near their\n;; entry. Silent when JOLT_TARGET is unset, so dual-host scripts (JVM leg) and\n;; legacy invocations keep their output byte-identical.\n(def child-print-snippet\n  \"(when-let [t (System/getenv \\\"JOLT_TARGET\\\")] (println (str \\\"jolt-target: \\\" t)))\")\n\n\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
